season misconduct allegations have not visibly dented his standing, and markets price Lee's chances near one in ten.", "watch": "Fresh, well-sourced allegations against Lamb or a sharp national shift toward Democrats could compress what otherwise looks like a double-digit Republican margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Andy Biggs (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Arizona's 5th District is a solidly Republican seat, and Andy Biggs's retirement to run for governor does not change its fundamentals. Mark Lamb, the Trump-backed nominee, won a contentious primary despite misconduct allegations and enters the general election with a large financial edge, having raised about $1.2 million plus outside support to Elizabeth Lee's roughly $150,000. No public general-election polls are available, so the picture rests on the district's ten-point Republican lean, the money gap, and expert judgment.\n\nEvery major rater places the race in the Safe or Solid Republican column, with one at Lean Republican, and prediction markets give Lee roughly a nine percent chance. Lee, who appeared alone at a Clean Elections debate, is running a low-budget campaign. The expectation is a comfortable double-digit Republican win, with modest residual uncertainty from Lamb's ethics questions and the lack of polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0636, "margin": -9.85}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0887, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0306, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.084, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0509, "margin": -11.0}]}